Multilayer Perceptron - Term Glossary
======================================

Neuron: a unit that multiplies its inputs by weights, sums them plus a bias, and passes the result through an activation function.
Weight: a number on a connection between two neurons that controls how much that input matters.
Bias: an extra learnable number added to a neuron's weighted sum, letting it shift its output independent of the inputs.
limit: To keep the random starting weights small enough that signals don't explode or vanish as they pass through the network's layers.
Layer: a group of neurons that share the same inputs and feed the same next layer.
Network / MLP: a stack of layers (input -> hidden -> ... -> output) where every neuron connects to every neuron in the next layer.
Activation function: a function applied to a neuron's output that lets the network represent non-linear patterns (e.g. sigmoid, softmax).
Forward pass: feeding an input through the network layer by layer to get a prediction.
Loss function: a single number measuring how wrong a prediction is.
Backpropagation: the algorithm that computes how much each weight/bias contributed to the loss, working backward from the output.
Gradient descent: the algorithm that nudges weights/biases to reduce the loss, using the gradients from backpropagation.
Learning rate: a number controlling how big each gradient descent update step is.
Epoch: one full pass of training over the entire training dataset.
Batch / batch size: a small chunk of the training data used for one weight update; its size is the batch size.
One-hot encoding: representing a category as a vector of 0s with a single 1 marking the class (e.g. B -> [1,0], M -> [0,1]).
Weight initialization: setting starting weight values to small random numbers so neurons don't all behave identically.
Random seed (rng): a fixed starting point for a random number generator so "random" results are reproducible.
Xavier / Glorot initialization: a rule for scaling random weight ranges based on the number of inputs, keeping signals stable across layers.
Normalization (min-max scaling): rescaling features to a common [0,1] range so no feature dominates just because its values are larger.
